GitHub Pages needs an EMPTY file named exactly:

    .nojekyll

(at the repo root, next to index.html)

Windows hides dotfiles, so this visible copy exists for upload workflows
that refuse ".nojekyll".

EASIEST — create it on github.com (no upload needed):
  1. Open your repo on GitHub
  2. Add file → Create new file
  3. Name the file:  .nojekyll
  4. Leave the file empty → Commit

OR — rename this file on GitHub after upload:
  1. Upload THIS file (dot-nojekyll.upload-me) any way you normally deploy
  2. On GitHub: open the file → ... → Rename → change to  .nojekyll
  3. Delete the old name if duplicate

OR — from PowerShell in this folder:
  Copy-Item dot-nojekyll.upload-me .nojekyll -Force
  git add -f .nojekyll
  git commit -m "Add .nojekyll for GitHub Pages"
  git push

Without .nojekyll, GitHub runs Jekyll and can drop or break static exploit files.
